Friction Log Entry: Local Webhook Ingestion & Handshake Verification

1. Specific Task Attempted:
Connecting a local development server (localhost:3000) to the Amazon Ring Developer Playground to receive and process real-time simulated webhook event payloads.

2. Steps Taken:
- Set up an endpoint route (/api/webhooks/ring) in our application to listen for inbound POST requests.
- Attempted to enter http://localhost:3000/api/webhooks/ring into the playground webhook callback settings.
- When localhost was rejected due to HTTPS requirements, launched an ngrok tunnel (https://<subdomain>.ngrok-free.app) and pointed the playground to the secure URL.
- Fired simulated device.motion_detected and package.detected triggers from the sandbox.

3. Expected vs. Actual Result:
- Expected: The sandbox dispatcher delivers the sample JSON payload to the endpoint with standard HMAC verification headers and provides visibility into the HTTP status code returned by the local server.
- Actual: The sandbox returned generic delivery failures ("502 / Delivery Failed") due to strict SSL handshake constraints and sandbox rate limits, without exposing the raw response body or detailed error codes to aid debugging.

4. Severity Rating:
Medium (slows onboarding and forces developers to spend time troubleshooting network configurations instead of building core features).

5. Workaround Used:
Built an internal client-side mock event dispatcher and JSON replay drawer inside the application that adheres directly to the Ring API specification, allowing offline and local testing without relying on external network dispatchers.

6. Actionable Suggestion:
Provide an official, zero-configuration local simulator bridge (like Stripe CLI's "stripe listen --forward-to" or AWS SAM local) that streams simulated Ring events directly to localhost without requiring public URLs or external tunneling.
